<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Çandarlı Halil Pasha, Halil Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Younger was grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1439 to 1453, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and in the first years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, the fourth of the Çandarlı family to hold the office. When Murad abdicated in favour of the young Mehmed in 1444, and again during the Buçuktepe rebellion of 1446, Halil Pasha brought Murad back to the throne, which left Mehmed with a lasting resentment. He opposed the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and was suspected of taking bribes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. After the city fell on 29 May 1453, Mehmed had him imprisoned and executed on 10 July, the first Ottoman grand vizier to be executed by a sultan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Çandarlı Halil Pasha was the fourth and penultimate member of the Çandarlı family to be grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. His father, Çandarlı Ibrahim Pasha the Elder, his uncle Çandarlı Ali Pasha and his grandfather Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Elder had held the post, and his own son, Çandarlı Ibrahim Pasha the Younger, would hold it later. The family had grown extremely wealthy from more than a century of influence, possibly more so than the ruling Ottoman house. Halil was appointed in 1439 after the deposition of Nizamüddin Pasha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, a man more interested in religion and the arts than politics, Halil Pasha enjoyed unlimited trust. Twice Murad retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a>, and Halil Pasha had a castle built in a nearby town for his protection, renaming it Çandarlı after his family; meanwhile Halil held effective control in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a> with the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> as nominal sultan. When Murad abdicated in 1444, Halil urged him to return; Murad marched against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-varna">Battle of Varna</a> on 14 November. After Murad again left the throne to Mehmed in 1445, Halil organised his return during the Buçuktepe rebellion of 1446. Murad remained sultan until his death in 1451. Halil ensured the immediate transfer of power to Mehmed.</p><p>Mehmed, deposed twice because of Halil, resented him. Halil opposed the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and was suspected of taking bribes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> sent a message asking for an increase of the annuity of Mehmed's cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a>, a possible pretender, or his release, Halil is recorded as answering that the schemes of the Byzantines would be fruitless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was taken on 29 May 1453. Mehmed imprisoned Halil Pasha on 1 June, and he was executed on 10 July 1453 and buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">İznik</a> in an open tomb without a roof, unlike his ancestors. He was the first Ottoman grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> executed by a sultan. The Turkish historian Y. Oztuna held that his execution ended the struggle between the Turkish aristocratic party and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> party, which the latter won. The Çandarlı family became provincial notables based in İznik, although his son later served briefly as grand vizier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Younger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Mehmed II had his grand vizier <b>Çandarlı Halil Pasha</b> executed in 1453, these posts went more and more to the sultan’s kul, his “slaves”: boys taken from Christian families by the devshirme, converted to Islam and trained in the palace school, some 300 of them inside Topkapı…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1443" aria-label="Istanbul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Çandarlı Halil Pasha the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1443">What was here in 1443? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
